17 (vi) The Government of Tamil Nadu and another vs. G.Nanda and another in A.S.No.423 and 925 of 2012;
https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis ( Uploaded on: 04/11/2025 09:30:40 pm ) and (vii) Ravinder Kaur Grewal and others vs Manjit Kaur and others reported in (2019) 8 SCC 729;
(viii) Thankappan vs. The State of Tamil Nadu and another reported in 2022 (2) CTC 19.
(s) On a careful perusal of those Judgments, it is clear that to prove the adverse possession, there should be concrete proof of open, hostile and continuous possession is required, Adverse possession must be adequate in continuously, in publicly and extent and a plaint is required at the least to show when possession becomes adverse so that the starting point of limitation against the party affected can be found.
